Capturing the manual's pictures
===============================

The game draws characters with colour attributes, so a picture of the screen can
be the screen: these are captured from the running game and emitted as SVG, in
Angband's own palette, at whatever size the reader zooms to.

    scripts/screenshot/take.sh <savefile-name>

The savefile is a character of your own, in the game's save directory. It is
never written to -- a copy is used.

**It regenerates every picture, against the character you give it.** The
existing ones were not all taken from the same character or the same world, and
their captions in docs/screenshots.rst describe what is in each -- the towns on
that world map, the services outside that village, the fish off that coast. So
running this with a different savefile rewrites four pictures the prose no
longer matches. To add or retake one, run it and then revert the others, or
take the single block you want out of the script.

How it works, in three steps:

  1. The test front end (-mtest) is driven headlessly with the character loaded.
     It prints every character, position and colour the game draws.
  2. capture.py replays that stream into a grid, honouring clears and wipes, and
     snapshots the screen each time the game asks for input.
  3. tosvg.py renders one of those frames, using the palette read straight out
     of src/z-color.c.

These environment variables set the scene, and each only does what the debug
menu already offers a player:

  ZTK_SHOT       know every place, so the world map is not mostly blank
  ZTK_SHOT_MAP   open the world map
  ZTK_SHOT_COAST stand the character on the nearest waterline
  ZTK_SHOT_LORE  know the whole bestiary
  ZTK_SHOT_MUTATE grant a fixed handful of mutations, for the sheet page that
                 lists them -- named rather than rolled, so the picture is the
                 same every time it is taken
  ZTK_SHOT_PETS  put four named pets beside the character, the same way the
                 debug menu's "Gain a pet" does, for the picture of the pet
                 menu.  Four because that is pets:max-carried
  ZTK_TERM_W/H   a terminal larger than a player's, so the whole world fits
  ZTK_SAVEFILE   let -mtest load a savefile at all

ZTK_SHOT_COAST rebuilds the surface at the coast it finds.  That has to happen
before the first level is built rather than on a later turn: asking for a new
level mid-turn is honoured by the full game loop and ignored by the headless
front end these are captured through, which cost an hour to work out once.

Menus can be captured too -- powers.svg is one.  Send `key C-[` twice after the
opening `key enter`s to close the command menu those leave open, then the key
for the command, then a few `noop`s for the menu to draw.

Regenerating a picture runs the current code, so these cannot drift from the
game the way a folder of PNGs does.
